# split.view: two zones side by side, each drawn by its own view in a column of its own.
#
# ONE way in, a decorator over two zones written back to back:
#
#   @{view:split}
#   ```mermaid
#   flowchart TD
#       A[source] --> B[render]
#   ```
#   > The text that explains the diagram,
#   > folded into the right-hand column.
#
# A side is any decorated zone (`@{view:box}` over its table, `@{view:banner}` over its quote),
# or a bare payload taking the view its shape names: a mermaid fence is `mermaid`, a markdown
# fence is `markdown`, a quote is `quote`. ONE blank line may part the two sides, and a quote written first needs it, since a
# quote ends on nothing else. A split is never a side: one cannot nest in another.
#
# NO BODY, on purpose. The carrier reads the two zones before any template loads and the engine
# lays them out (layout/aside.ts): each side starts on half the width, a side narrower than its
# half hands the rest to the other, and the taller side is asked again under the other's height,
# which only a diagram answers. Too narrow for two columns, the sides stack, each at full width.
#
# Where this hook does not run, the decorator line is one harmless line of prose and each side
# shows as it would alone: the diagram draws itself and the quote is a quote.
